  "query_preview": "..\n(Today through next Thursday)\n\nWEATHER PATTERN OVERVIEW: Cut off low remains poised over NorCal \nthis morning before ejecting east along the northern Nevada border \nFriday. The position of the low over NorCal today and tomorrow \nwill produce elevated south- southwesterly winds across most of \nthe Great Basin today, especially across central Nevada. The \nstrongest winds will be focused across White Pine County and gusts\n30 to 35 MPH, reaching 45 MPH across the high terrain should be \nexpected. Combined with low relative humidity, this has prompted \na Red Flag Warning this afternoon and evening for portions of \ncentral Nevada. Patchy blowing dust will also be present. Breezes \nwill be present Friday, though winds are expected to be more \nmodest than today. The upper low and associated weak surface \nboundary will not produce significant rainfall with atmospheric \nmoisture being the limiting factor. However, that being said, \nlocations along the Idaho and Oregon borders will observe isolated\nto scattered light rain showers or along with isolated dry \nthunderstorms today and Friday. Concurrently, significant moisture\nacross Utah, thanks to a northerly push of subtropical moisture \nfrom the desert southwest, will spill into portions of eastern \nNevada and will also produce isolated to scattered rain showers \nand isolated dry thunderstorms along the Utah state line. \n\nAs the cut off low ejects east along the Nevada border Friday \nmorning, moisture will also be pushed east out of the forecast \narea, producing dry weather and generally light winds for this \nweekend. Temperatures across northern Nevada will cool several \ndegrees Thursday night and Friday as the low and associated cooler\nair mass move east. Temperatures gradually warm thru the weekend \nwith a return to daytime highs in the upper 70s to mid 80s across \nthe valleys by Sunday. A low chance (10%) for rain showers across \ncentral Nevada will be present early next week as a weak Pacific \nlow pressure system moves into central California from the west. \n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS: High confidence of critical fire\nweather conditions this afternoon within portions of central \nNevada due to elevated winds and low relative humidity. Moderate \nconfidence exists for showers and thunderstorms along the northern\nand eastern NV borders today and tomorrow. Significant grid edits\nrequired within PoP grids to better represent thunderstorm \nchances today and tomorrow.",
